# Scaling and Dimension
#
# How quantities change with size, gathered from dimensional analysis, transport, reactors, animals, polymers, critical points, perception, and Spw handle units, with each pairing naming where it breaks.

#>concepts_scaling_and_dimension
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!concepts
#:form #!comparison
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Scaling and Dimension"
 ~#claim: "Dimensions fix how many independent ratios a law has, and power laws show how size changes the rules, but exponents from geometry, fixed points, and fits mean different things."
 ~#proof: "Buckingham and Bridgman count the ratios, Galileo and Thompson give the square-cube argument, Kleiber and Glazier the measured exponents, and Wilson the universal ones."
 ~#door: "Try the two cups, then read the square-cube pairing and its break."
 ~#audience: "physics and engineering students, biologists, and designers who resize things"
 continuity: "Scaling and Dimension | size changes the rules"
 cold_open: "Double every length and the volume grows eightfold while the surface grows fourfold, so size changes the rules."
}

